

Assume arrays of accurate sizes past_blues, past_reds, anticone_blues for each block

Assume an array L of lower bounds of red anticone sizes

Invariant: 
    L[i] ≤ |(G \ U) ⋂ anticone(i)| = |anticone_reds(i)|

Definition:

score_i =  |U ⋂ future(i)|                              - |(G \ U) ⋂ future(i)|
        =  |future_blues(i)|                            - |future_reds(i)|
        =  |U| - past_blues[i] - anticone_blues[i] - 1  - (|(G \ U)| - past_reds[i] - |anticone_reds(i)| - 1)
        =  |U| - past_blues[i] - anticone_blues[i]      - (|(G \ U)| - past_reds[i] - |anticone_reds(i)|)
        ≥  |U| - past_blues[i] - anticone_blues[i]      - (|(G \ U)| - past_reds[i] - L[i])

Denote the score lower bound:

floor_i := |U| - past_blues[i] - anticone_blues[i]      - (|(G \ U)| - past_reds[i] - L[i])

-> floor_i := - past_blues[i] - anticone_blues[i]      + (past_reds[i] + L[i])
           := antifuture_reds - antifuture_blues

So
    floor_i ≤ score_i

Observe that floor[i] is accessible via arrays

---------------------------------------------------

Let vote_i be the result of the real vote algorithm

Note that vote_i ≤ score_i (because blues can turn negative but not vice versa)


Observation: if floor_i ≥ -d for all i, then we are done

Proof:
    There are no negative blues so vote_i = score_i ≥ floor_i ≥ -d


---------------------------------------------------

Algorithm 1:
    let H denote a heap of floor values  
    let N = 0 // Negative blues counter
    loop:
        let f_i = pop min floor value from H // i is the block index
        if f_i < -d:
            calc score_i accurately and update L[i]
            if f_i increased:
                push updated f to H
            else:
                N = N + 1

---

# Voting Algorithm:

let H denote a heap of floor values  
let S denote a secondary heap of floor values for negative blues popped from H
let N = 0 // Negative blues counter
let threshold = total_red_work - total_blue_work + deficit

loop:
    let f_i = pop min floor value from H // i is the block index

    if f_i >= -threshold + N:
        return true
    
    calc score_i accurately and update L[i]
        > by reading the new reds from red_set starting from red_set[(f_i.red_index + 1)..=(red_set.length - 1)]
    if f_i increased:
        push updated f to H
    else:
        N = N + 1
        S.push(f_i)
    
    if H is empty:
        return false

# Definition
all_tips - the full set of tips for the current block we are running DK for
subgroup - a group of tips that agree with each other in this conflict
- in code, the next chain ancestor after conflict genesis is the same for all these blocks

virtual - the virtual conditioned on agreeing with this subgroup. The conditioned VSPC (cVSPC) from this virtual will give the coloring
for the conflict zone

mcb - merging chain block

# Calculation of Counters

## Counters
past_blue_work
anticone_blue_work
past_red_work_upper_bound
anticone_red_work_lower_bound

### past_blue_work

1. if a block B is chain block in the cVSPC, it's past_blue_work counters are exactly correct from cVSPC's POV
   so: B.past_blue_work can be used as is
2. else:
   B.past_blue_work = mcb.past_blue_work - future_B_work_in_mcb_mergeset_blues - mcb.blues_anticone_work[B] - work(B)

### anticone_blue_work
virtual contains blues_anticone_work, similar to blues_anticone_sizes which is maintained when inheriting the selected parent's POV

procedure:
  anticone_work_map = map{}
  // coloring_data
  cd = virtual_coloring_data

  while not conflict_genesis:
    for key in cd.blues_anticone_work:
      if key not in anticone_work_map:
        anticone_work_map[key] = cd.blues_anticone_work[key]

    cd = cd.selected_parent.coloring_data

rationale:

blues_anticone_work is maintained when interiting the SP's POV. The latest coloring data has the most up to date total value for the entire zone
any missing blue's data can be collected through their first occurrence in the cVSPC.

### past_red_work_upper_bound and anticone_red_lower_bound

Again, for cVSPC chain blocks, these values are exact (or exactly calculable)

Due to the presence of "gray blocks", it's not very easy to calculate past_red_work efficiently without doing O(R) amount of work. However, instead
of attempting to calculate past_red_work exactly, we can settle for a past_red_work_upper_bound where this value may contain work from the anticone.

This is acceptable as long as the past_red_work_upper_bound + anticone_red_lower_bound == past_red_work_exact + anticone_red_lower_bound'
(meaning, we don't double count an anticone block)
